# {{ product_name }}

Generated from [prodij/copio](https://github.com/prodij/copio). Update with `copier update --trust`.

    make env          # writes .env (git-ignored) with generated local secrets
    make up           # starts the stack
    make bootstrap    # configures Zitadel, Lago and (with a key) Stripe; safe to run again
    open http://{{ domain }}:5173    # 5173 is the default APP_PORT; use yours if you changed it

Stripe is optional. Without a key you can sign up, create a workspace and use the app, but billing
isn't set up: `POST /billing/checkout` answers 501 with that message, Billing says so, and paid features
stay off (`require_feature` still answers 402). To add it, paste a Stripe TEST secret key (sk_test_ only,
not rk_test_: https://dashboard.stripe.com/test/apikeys) into `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=` in `.env` and run
`make bootstrap` again. `make up` alone is not enough: it gives the backend the key, but Lago has no
Stripe provider until `make bootstrap` adds it, and new workspaces fail until then. Workspaces created
before the key get their Stripe customer at their first checkout.

## Mock payment (local tests with no Stripe account)

Mock payment fakes only the payment step, so `make e2e` can prove sign-up to premium with no Stripe key.
It is off by default. To turn it on, keep `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` empty, set `BILLING_MODE=mock` and
`APP_ENV=local` in `.env`, then `make up && make bootstrap && make e2e`.

- The backend won't start, and `make bootstrap` stops, with `BILLING_MODE=mock` and any other `APP_ENV`, or with a
  Stripe key set. No request can turn it on.
- Real Zitadel, real Lago and Lago's real signed webhook stay in the path. Lago gets a subscription with no
  payment rule and no provider, makes it active and sends `subscription.started`. Coming back to the app grants
  nothing: `require_feature` answers 402 until that webhook lands. Stripe is never called and `stripe-cli` stays idle.
- The app shows a "Mock payment" banner on every page. Lago's invoice stays unpaid, because nothing pays it.
- **A mock payment pass is not a Stripe pass.** The Stripe payment test is reported as skipped. The mock run does
  not prove Stripe's page, saved cards, Lago's payment-gated subscription, or what happens when a payment fails.
- `billing_reconcile` also activates mock subscriptions: it asks Lago every 15 minutes and when the scheduler
  starts, and Lago says active with nothing paid. So without the webhook, premium stays off only until its next
  run. That is why the test stops the job while it checks that only the webhook grants access.
- The test pauses `lago-worker` and stops `jobs`, and puts them back afterwards. The next mock payment `make e2e`
  repairs a killed run too; to do it by hand: `docker compose unpause lago-worker && docker compose start jobs`.
- Leaving mock payment: `make reset`. `make bootstrap` stops until you do, since the subscriptions it made would
  look real. It also stops when turning mock payment on while Lago has a Stripe provider, and checkout refuses a
  workspace whose Lago customer has one, because those can reach Stripe.

Host ports are set in `.env` (defaults: app 5173, API 8000, Zitadel 8085, its login page 3100,
Lago API 3000 and UI 8088, Postgres 5432, S3 (SeaweedFS) 8333). Every port is published on 127.0.0.1 only.

## Running two products

Generate each product in its own folder with its own `product_slug` and `domain`, then run the same three
commands in each. Nothing else needs setting:

    make env && make up && make bootstrap

- `make env` checks the default ports. If any is taken, it moves all of them up by the same step (10, then 20...),
  says so, and writes them to `.env`. `make bootstrap` prints the URLs to open.
- The Compose project is named from the slug, so containers, volumes, networks and built images are the product's
  own. `.bootstrap/` and `.env` live in the product's folder. Browsers keep each `*.localhost` domain's sign-ins
  and cookies apart.
- `make env` sees only what is running now. If it ran for both before either started, or the other product was
  down, both can hold the same ports and the second `make up` fails with `port is already allocated`. To fix it:
  `make down` in that product, empty the `*_PORT=` values in its `.env`, then `make env && make up && make bootstrap`.
  Moving a port later works the same way, and `make bootstrap` is what brings Zitadel's redirect URIs along.

## Updating from the template

Commit first, because Copier refuses a dirty tree. Then:

    uvx copier update --trust --defaults
    git status                 # UU means conflict markers to resolve; nothing is dropped silently
    make regen                 # rebuilds lock files, the brand CSS and the API contract
    make check

- `.env`, `.bootstrap/` and your own files are never touched. Your edits merge with the template's. Where both
  changed the same lines you get `<<<<<<< before updating` (yours) and `>>>>>>> after updating` (the template's).
- A lock file with conflict markers: `git checkout HEAD -- <lock file>`, resolve `pyproject.toml` or
  `package.json`, then `make regen`, which adds what the template wanted.
- Never edit these by hand, and an update leaves them alone: `frontend/src/brand.css`, `frontend/public/logo.svg`,
  `frontend/public/favicon.svg` and the title in `frontend/index.html` come from `brand/` (`make brand`);
  `backend/openapi.json` and `frontend/src/api/schema.d.ts` come from the routes (`make regen`). Don't edit
  `.copier-answers.yml` either: Copier needs its commit to work out what changed.
- Yours to edit, with the logo kept across updates: `brand/`, `backend/app/billing/plans.yaml`, this README, and
  your own routes, tables and pages (keep the `starter` plan's code, or update the tests that use it). Editing the template's own code (`scripts/`, `docker-compose.yml`, the billing and
  auth modules) works, but expect conflicts there; send generic fixes back to the template.

## Commands

- `make e2e` runs the Playwright tests against the running stack (after `make bootstrap`): sign-up to
  `/plans` always, sign-up to premium when `.env` has a Stripe key (reported as skipped without one), and
  sign-up to premium by mock payment when `.env` has `BILLING_MODE=mock`. `make check` runs lint, types and unit tests (needs Postgres: `make up`).
- Name, colours, font and logo live in `brand/brand.yaml`; they reach the app, the Zitadel login
  page and Lago when `make brand` runs (`make bootstrap` runs it too). `font` is only a name: to load it
  everywhere, set `font_file` too. Without it the app falls back to the bundled Inter unless that font is installed.
- The Zitadel console (`http://auth.{{ domain }}:8085/ui/console`, with your ZITADEL_PORT) signs in
  as `admin@{{ domain }}` with `ZITADEL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` from `.env`; the Lago UI as
  `LAGO_ORG_USER_EMAIL` with `LAGO_ORG_USER_PASSWORD`.
- `make doctor` lists stuck workspaces, checkouts and webhooks; `make reset` deletes all local data.
